Since a signal is function, we give an imperative description of the signal exactly as we did for functions. We give a procedure that has the potential of providing values for $s(t)$ given any t.

Example 2.14: We could define a Java method as follows:

```java

double s(double t) {

    return (Math.sin(440*2*Math.PI*t));

}

```

Calling this method with a value for t as an argument yields a value for s (t). Java (and most object-oriented languages) use the term "method" for most procedures.

Another alternative is to provide a set of samples of the signal.

Example 2.15: In Matlab, we could define a vector t that gives the values of time that we are interested in:

t = [0:1/8000:1] ;

In the vector t there are 8001 values evenly spaced between 0 and 1, so our sampling rate is 8000 samples per second. Then we can compute values of s for these values of t and listen to the resulting sound:

s = cos(2*pi*440*t);

sound(s,8000)

The vector s also has 8001 elements, representing evenly spaced samples of one second of A-440.
